Ques26:  so you are on client call , client disccus the requirement and and need the solution in some way , however you know it will create certain challenges in future, so
how you will convience the client on the same , client is not the technical person?
An26: When handling a non-technical client who is set on a specific implementation path, the key is to never frame the discussion around code or technical jargon. If you tell them "This will hit Apex CPU governor limits or create database locks," they won't grasp the impact and may think you are just resisting extra work.
Instead, frame the conversation around Business Impact, User Experience (UX), and Cost/Risk.
Here is the 4-step framework to handle this conversation effectively:

Step 1: Validate Their Intent First (Build Trust)
Never start by saying "No" or "That's a bad design." Acknowledge the business outcome they are trying to achieve.
What to say:
"I completely understand why you want this feature to work this way. Your primary goal is to make sure the operations team gets their customer data instantly without manual steps, and that makes complete business sense."

Step 2: Use Real-World Analogies (Translate the Risk)
Avoid technical terms. Translate the future technical debt into tangible business risks: Speed, System Downtime, or Operational Disruption.
If the issue is synchronous callouts/processing:
"Think of your current request like asking one person to run to five different stores across town while a customer waits on hold on the phone. Today, with 10 customers, that person might manage. But when you scale to 500 customers next quarter, the line will stall, screens will freeze, and your reps will see loading wheels or errors during live calls."
If the issue is excessive customization instead of standard configuration:
"Building this as a fully custom piece today is like buying a custom-built car part rather than a standard manufacturer part. It works today, but every time Salesforce releases an update or your team wants a small tweak, it will require a developer to fix it, which slows down future requests and increases maintenance costs."

Step 3: Present Options, Not Just an Objection
A senior architect never leaves the client with an open problem. Always present two clear, business-driven alternatives:
Option A (Their Requested Way):

What it costs: Fast to deliver today, but high risk of system slowdowns, rep frustration during peak hours, and higher rework costs in 6 months.

Option B (The Recommended Architectural Pattern):

What it delivers: Provides the exact same business outcome, but runs reliably in the background, keeps user screens fast, and easily scales as transaction volume triples.

Step 4: Validate with a Quick POC (Proof of Concept)
Non-technical stakeholders believe what they can see. Offer a quick demonstration rather than a theoretical debate.

What to say:
"As an architect in a client-facing role, I translate technical constraints into business realities. Non-technical clients care about three things: 
user adoption, system reliability, and cost. When a requested design will cause future bottlenecks, I first validate their business objective, use a practical operational analogy 
to explain the risk—like system freezing during peak hours—and then present a scalable alternative that achieves the exact same business result. 
If there's still hesitation, I build a quick POC to show them the real-time user experience, which almost always secures their buy-in."

